    "seminar_id": "qep",
    "seminar_name": "Quantitative Economic Policy Seminar",
    "seminar_page": "https://www.old.wiwi.uni-frankfurt.de/abteilungen/eq/seminars/quantitative-economic-policy-seminar.html",
    "title": "Deforestation and structural change – The case of tourism in Brazil",
    "speaker": "Manuel Linsenmeier (Goethe University Frankfurt)",
    "speaker_url": "https://mlinsenmeier.com/",
    "date": "2026-07-16",
    "raw_date": "16 July 2026",
    "time_info": "14:00–15:15",
    "start_time": "14:00",
    "end_time": "15:15",
    "location": "RuW 4.202",
    "description": "Abstract:\nDeforestation in the tropics is a major driver of climate change. This paper studies the role of local structural change for reducing deforestation in developing countries and emerging economies. The paper focuses on tourism in Brazil as a case study. The paper combines an empirical econometric analysis with a quantitative spatial model, utilizing a granular and rich dataset that includes satellite imagery, individual-level census micro-data, establishment-level tourism data, and administrative social insurance data, among other sources. Our findings indicate that domestic and international tourism in Brazil substantially increased employment in tourism-related industries but lowered local wages, with its employment effects spilling over into other industries and nearby locations. Furthermore, simulations with our calibrated model suggest that tourism-related traded services reduced deforestation by providing local employment opportunities outside agriculture, including in the Amazon region. Overall, our results suggest that tourism helps to preserve natural land equivalent to the total deforested area over the last 20 years, illustrating the potential environmental benefits of local structural change.",
    "description_html": "<p style=\"line-height: 1.428571;\"><strong>Abstract: </strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: Helvetica;\"></span></p>\n<p>Deforestation in the tropics is a major driver of climate change. This paper studies the role of local structural change for reducing deforestation in developing countries and emerging economies. The paper focuses on tourism in Brazil as a case study. The paper combines an empirical econometric analysis with a quantitative spatial model, utilizing a granular and rich dataset that includes satellite imagery, individual-level census micro-data, establishment-level tourism data, and administrative social insurance data, among other sources. Our findings indicate that domestic and international tourism in Brazil substantially increased employment in tourism-related industries but lowered local wages, with its employment effects spilling over into other industries and nearby locations. Furthermore, simulations with our calibrated model suggest that tourism-related traded services reduced deforestation by providing local employment opportunities outside agriculture, including in the Amazon region. Overall, our results suggest that tourism helps to preserve natural land equivalent to the total deforested area over the last 20 years, illustrating the potential environmental benefits of local structural change.</p><div><p><span style=\"color: rgb(24, 25, 27);\"><br/></span></p></div>",
    "details_url": "https://www.old.wiwi.uni-frankfurt.de/abteilungen/eq/seminars/quantitative-economic-policy-seminar/calendar-details/cal/event/tx_cal_phpicalendar/2026/07/16/2168.html",
    "source": "Goethe University Frankfurt"
  },
  {
    "seminar_id": "lawfin",
    "seminar_name": "LawFin Research Seminar",
    "seminar_page": "https://www.lawfin.uni-frankfurt.de/events/lawfin-research-seminars",
    "title": "Stung into Action? Cyber Risk Management After a Breach",
    "speaker": "Emina Ademovic",
    "speaker_url": "https://eminaademovic.com/",
    "date": "2026-09-07",
    "raw_date": "07 Sep 2026",
    "time_info": "14:15–15:30",
    "start_time": "14:15",
    "end_time": "15:30",
    "location": "HoF 1.01 + online",
    "description": "Hybrid event.\nTo enter the virtual seminar room, please use the following login credentials:\nZoom URL:\nhttps://uni-frankfurt.zoom-x.de/j/69051662513?pwd=ylK2ozLt3090SNTwVFKUjb8DT0EV8S.1\nMeeting ID: 690 5166 2513\nPassword: 490462\nAbstract:\nWe study how cyber incidents reshape firms’ incentives to invest in cybersecurity and whether these investments pay off. Using novel measures from job postings and employee records, we document large and persistent post-incident improvements that move affected firms ahead of comparable firms in both cybersecurity human capital and technology. Firms with stronger pre-incident in-house cybersecurity capabilities experience smaller negative abnormal returns when incidents occur, whereas firms with greater reliance on external cloud infrastructure experience larger losses. Incident disclosure induces precautionary cybersecurity investment among similarly exposed firms, though firms' responses also raise reliance on common providers, contributing to systemic risk.",
    "description_html": "<p>Hybrid event.</p>\n<p>To enter the virtual seminar room, please use the following login credentials: </p>\n<p><strong>Zoom URL: </strong><a href=\"https://uni-frankfurt.zoom-x.de/j/69051662513?pwd=ylK2ozLt3090SNTwVFKUjb8DT0EV8S.1\" rel=\"noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://uni-frankfurt.zoom-x.de/j/69051662513?pwd=ylK2ozLt3090SNTwVFKUjb8DT0EV8S.1</a><br/><strong>Meeting ID: 690 5166 2513</strong><br/><strong>Password: 490462</strong><br/><br/>Abstract:</p>\n<p><i>We study how cyber incidents reshape firms’ incentives to invest in cybersecurity and whether these investments pay off. Using novel measures from job postings and employee records, we document large and persistent post-incident improvements that move affected firms ahead of comparable firms in both cybersecurity human capital and technology. Firms with stronger pre-incident in-house cybersecurity capabilities experience smaller negative abnormal returns when incidents occur, whereas firms with greater reliance on external cloud infrastructure experience larger losses. Incident disclosure induces precautionary cybersecurity investment among similarly exposed firms, though firms' responses also raise reliance on common providers, contributing to systemic risk.</i></p>",
    "details_url": "https://www.lawfin.uni-frankfurt.de/events/event?tx_calendarize_calendar%5Baction%5D=detail&tx_calendarize_calendar%5Bcontroller%5D=Calendar&tx_calendarize_calendar%5Bindex%5D=303&cHash=c93d8b484b3c83d71e969226c7c48f0b",
    "source": "LawFin Goethe University Frankfurt"
